WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:02.780
- [Narrator] This chip controls your car,

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:05.560
stores data about what you
bought and runs the software

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:07.140
on your laptop.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:10.100
These chips have come to power
our lives, and only a handful

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:13.460
of fabrication plants or fabs
in the world have the know-how

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:15.430
and infrastructure to make them.

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:18.050
So most of the chips are
produced outside of the US

00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:19.760
like at this fab in Singapore

00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:24.050
- The bill of fab is a big investment.

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:25.940
- [Narrator] And now the
world is running out of chips

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:28.430
because companies can't
make them fast enough.

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:30.120
- When order really flooding in.

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:31.930
We had to turn on every single piece

00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:34.720
of machine that we can
find in the factory.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:36.270
- [Narrator] The pandemic
rattled supply chains

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:38.420
and led to a surge in
demand for electronics

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:40.200
when people were stuck at home.

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.610
While a perfect storm
of natural disasters,

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:45.500
a fire at one of the world's
leading auto chip makers

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:48.590
and the ongoing US-China trade
war disrupted the production

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:50.820
and distribution of semiconductors.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:52.960
This shortage has affected
whether you can drive a Jeep

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:55.423
off the lot or buy a new PlayStation 5.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:58.860
We visit one of the world's
largest contract chip makers

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:00.610
to see the complex process

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:03.110
and why there's no quick
fix to the supply crunch.

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:08.510
- Chip usually it takes about
two to three months to make.

00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:10.680
- [Narrator] Daniel Rajkumar
is a manufacturing manager

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:13.440
at one of the fabs at Global Foundries.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:15.760
The American company is
the third largest contract

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:17.730
chip manufacturer in the world.

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:20.320
Companies like Intel, AMD and Bosch

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:23.410
give some of their circuitry
designs to Global Foundries.

00:01:23.410 --> 00:01:25.460
And then it's fabs like
this one in Singapore

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:27.420
manufacture the chips.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:31.170
- Once we finish the
fabrication of the circuitry,

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:32.760
it has to go for testing.

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:34.940
It has to go for repackaging.

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:36.980
- [Narrator] Altogether it
could take as much as six months

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:39.860
before a chip is ready
to go into your tech.

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:41.450
This long process starts here,

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:44.070
at the center of the fab
called the clean room.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:45.540
- Before going into the clean room,

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:46.950
we need to gown up.

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:48.970
- [Narrator] Because chips can
be as small as a fingernail

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:51.170
and crammed with billions of components,

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:53.270
they have to be handled with great care.

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:56.170
- Any form of dust that
falls onto the wafers

00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:58.370
you will cause the chip
to be the defective.

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:02.913
And then now we are able
to go into the air shower.

00:02:02.913 --> 00:02:04.140
Okay.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:05.830
- [Narrator] Global Foundries
says this clean room

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:08.380
is a thousand times cleaner
than an operating room.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:13.260
And it also takes up a lot of space.

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:14.880
- Two footballs field.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:16.340
That's how big it is.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:19.570
We have about seven to 800 machines.

00:02:19.570 --> 00:02:20.680
- [Narrator] It's also yellow in here

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:23.080
because chips are sensitive to UV rays.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:24.830
And this lighting has none of that.

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:27.940
- This is always the first step.

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:29.550
- [Narrator] The starter
material for any chip

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:31.400
is here in this room.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:33.710
These super thin disks are called wafers

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:35.490
and made from silicon.

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:37.970
Eventually one of these
will produce about 1000

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:40.340
to 1500 individual chips.

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:42.390
But before it does, the wafer is placed

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:43.940
in a special container.

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:45.820
- We have the raw silicon.

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:48.610
We are now trying to
register this material

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:51.840
and translate into our wafer carrier.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.290
- [Narrator] Each carrier
can hold up to 25 wafers

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:56.230
and there are about 4,000 of these

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:58.280
moving around the facility at one time.

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:01.030
- We are also the source of particles

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:02.910
when we go into the clean room.

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:04.740
- [Narrator] So never
once during manufacturing

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:07.630
does the wafer come in contact
with any of the workers.

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:10.920
- You will bring the wafers
directly to the machine.

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:14.580
And in this foundry, basically 95%

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:18.130
of all processing jobs is fully automated.

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:19.420
- [Narrator] These raw wafers are cleaned

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:22.090
before starting a process
that Rajkumar describes

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:24.210
as similar to making layered cake.

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:27.150
- The main function of diffusion cleaners

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:32.150
is basically to go layers of
oxide or to dap the nitrite

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:34.000
onto the silicon.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:36.050
- [Narrator] That creates
a protective coating.

00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:38.680
Next is a layer that makes
the wafer light-sensitive.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:40.840
So it's ready for one of
the most important steps

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:42.440
called lithography.

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:46.140
- This is the most expensive
module in any wafer foundry.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:47.900
The number of lithography machine

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:50.330
will define how many
wafers you can produce

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:52.180
in a month or a year.

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:54.130
- [Narrator] One lithography
machine can cost anywhere

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:57.590
between 25 million to
over 100 million dollars.

00:03:57.590 --> 00:03:59.080
And that's because it's responsible

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:01.090
for adding layers of the circuitry.

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:03.520
Basically electronic
components like transistors

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:06.370
and diodes that allow the
chips eventually store data

00:04:06.370 --> 00:04:08.110
or run apps.

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:11.960
- What I'm holding here is
basically a (indistinct) design

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:13.080
of a circuitry.

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:15.200
- [Narrator] Inside this
pink box is a photo mask,

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:16.830
which is like a glass stencil.

00:04:16.830 --> 00:04:20.090
And clients send these
directly to Global Foundries.

00:04:20.090 --> 00:04:22.160
The lithography machine blasts UV light

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:23.980
through the photo mask and prints patterns

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:26.230
on the wafer over and over again.

00:04:26.230 --> 00:04:29.430
The difference between an
advanced chip for a 5G smartphone

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:31.218
versus one for your credit card comes down

00:04:31.218 --> 00:04:33.870
to the type of wavelength that's used.

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:36.380
Shorter wavelengths mean
you can edge finer features

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:38.900
and get more performance out of the chips.

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:41.040
Some of these transmitters are so small

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:42.610
that they're measured in nanometers

00:04:42.610 --> 00:04:45.250
and compared to DNA strands.

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:47.100
Once the circuitry pattern is imprinted,

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:49.170
the wafer has to be charged.

00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:51.420
- These machines use a lot of energy

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:54.000
to produce electrical charge

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:57.140
that allows the electrical
to flow to the chip

00:04:57.140 --> 00:05:00.020
and also creates the
different type of function

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:01.950
for your electrical chips.

00:05:01.950 --> 00:05:03.990
- Rajkumar says many of
these steps are repeated

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:06.920
hundreds of times before
the fabrication is complete.

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:08.440
And to get to this point,

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:11.530
these orders have to be made
at least a year in advance.

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:14.380
- I have a demo wafers here.

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:15.700
- [Narrator] The finished
wafers are tested

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:18.470
then finally sliced into individual chips.

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:20.320
- One is this small die wafers,

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:23.220
which is mainly useful
bank cards or chips.

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:24.600
And then you have the other one

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:26.070
which is the bigger die wafers

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:28.430
which can go into other
kinds of applications

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:32.380
like computer processing chips
or electrical appliances.

00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:34.370
- [Narrator] Global Foundry
says it's fab at Singapore

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:38.000
typically makes about
600,000 wafers a year.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:39.800
But with the recent surge in demand,

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:43.040
it's making around 120,000 more.

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:44.570
- This whole year is fully booked out.

00:05:44.570 --> 00:05:47.670
We don't have any more
space for new customers.

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:49.380
- [Narrator] Tan Yew Kong
is the vice president

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:52.840
and general manager of Global
Foundries Fab 7 in Singapore.

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:55.020
And he says, there's no quick solution.

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:56.810
- There's no space that you can house

00:05:56.810 --> 00:05:59.200
another lithography
tool or any other tools

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:00.240
that you'd like to buy.

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:05.060
It is a (indistinct) easily,
a one year to 15 months

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:09.250
before you can see a tool that
is coming into your factory.

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:11.110
- [Narrator] It's not just
a backlog of equipment,

00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:13.370
but Tan says expanding or building a fab

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:16.330
from the ground up would
require deep investments.

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:19.190
More than the $1.4 billion
that Global Foundries

00:06:19.190 --> 00:06:21.350
has already committed to spend this year

00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:24.220
on expanding its three
fabs around the world.

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:28.410
- You easily need to spend
7 billion to 15 billion

00:06:28.410 --> 00:06:31.090
depending on the size of the
factory you want to build

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:33.270
and the technology that
you're gonna develop

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:36.903
can easily take you decades
to build the foundations.

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:39.300
- [Narrator] So to fill as many orders

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:40.440
as quickly as possible,

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:42.840
the company turned on idle machines

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:45.460
and opened this factory control tower.

00:06:45.460 --> 00:06:48.650
Engineers here have a bird's-eye
view of the entire fab.

00:06:48.650 --> 00:06:50.960
And the green boxes show
which machines are running

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:52.520
at max capacity.

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:53.570
Before this control tower

00:06:53.570 --> 00:06:54.860
when there was a problem,

00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:56.500
engineers had to sort through data

00:06:56.500 --> 00:06:59.020
and piece together information themselves.

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:02.020
- Compared to today, just
looking at the screen

00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:04.930
you already know that this
is a piece of machine.

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:06.780
You need to pay attention now.

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:09.140
- [Narrator] Tan says any
time saved is precious

00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:11.010
but to truly address the shortage,

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:13.440
the company is working more
closely with its clients,

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:15.053
suppliers and the government.

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:19.360
- We're going to continue
seeing more government support

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:21.980
because it is becoming
such an important part

00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:24.040
of the entire global supply chain.

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:26.940
- There's no reason why
Americans should wait.

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:28.200
We're investing aggressively

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:31.200
in areas like semiconductors
and batteries.

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:32.350
- [Narrator] The Biden administration

00:07:32.350 --> 00:07:34.040
has proposed $50 billion

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:36.330
to boost America's chip production.

00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:38.370
That's because while the
US is one of the largest

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:41.160
semiconductor markets, the
majority of the complex

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:43.990
and expensive manufacturing
happens at a few companies

00:07:43.990 --> 00:07:46.370
based in China and East Asia.

00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:49.180
- That's because the world's
biggest foundry in the world,

00:07:49.180 --> 00:07:51.430
TSMC is located in Taiwan.

00:07:51.430 --> 00:07:54.080
Samsung also has a sizeable foundry

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:56.590
as well as some Chinese players.

00:07:56.590 --> 00:07:59.290
- [Narrator] This concentration
of fabs means any disruption

00:07:59.290 --> 00:08:01.870
to the supply chain,
like a drought in Taiwan.

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:03.640
Or political tensions with China

00:08:03.640 --> 00:08:05.540
can have big ripple effects.

00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:07.930
Especially when global
demand is expected to grow

00:08:07.930 --> 00:08:10.500
by more than 12% this year.

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:14.740
- We have technologies like 5G
and artificial intelligence.

00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:16.460
And these types of advanced technology

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:18.920
means that more chips would be needed

00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:22.250
to operate the devices
that we use every day

00:08:22.250 --> 00:08:23.400
- [Narrator] To meet that demand,

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:24.940
the Chip Industry Association

00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:28.380
says it will require an
investment of $3 trillion globally

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:29.993
over the next three decades.

00:08:31.550 --> 00:08:34.730
So for us to buy new devices,
Tan says his fab will work

00:08:34.730 --> 00:08:35.563
around the clock

00:08:35.563 --> 00:08:38.490
as it turns this challenge
into a business opportunity.

00:08:38.490 --> 00:08:41.630
- From the ambitions of
wanting to grow the company,

00:08:41.630 --> 00:08:44.780
this is definitely a
positive point for us.

00:08:44.780 --> 00:08:47.863
This over demand is gonna
be around for a while.

